 1 big thing: The all-about-me campaign 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Saul Loeb/AFP via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump is hitting the campaign trail and doing the last thing many Republicans want: making the 59 days to the Nov. 3 midterms a referendum on him, Axios' Marc Caputo reports. 

 Why it matters : Most Republicans are too scared to bluntly tell Trump how unpopular he is. Timidity aside, Republicans need Trump's low-propensity voters to turn out. So they're going along with a Trump-focused strategy that many believe could cost them credibility — along with seats in the House and Senate. "We have no choice. We're stuck on the boat with him whether we like it or not," said a top Republican campaign official who's in contact with Trump's political operation.

 "Now, a rising tide raises all boats," the official added. "But the only ones who sound sure about the tide rising is the White House." 🐘 Coming attractions: At Trump's urging, the GOP on Wednesday and Thursday will host an unprecedented midterm convention in Dallas, headlined by the president. 

 Trump plans to appear both nights , with Vice President JD Vance as keynote on Wednesday and Trump on Thursday. 📱 Trump is also the star of his political operation's first two TV ads:

 The first 30-second ad, "Toughest Guy," is all about you-know-who. The second, "Tax Cut Prices," features Trump and would be more conventional for a campaign in which he's on the ballot. 📺 Inside the room: Trump essentially demanded the ads focus on him because midterms are referendums on a president anyway, and he wanted to tout his accomplishments, The Atlantic first reported ( gift link ).

 "I'm not on the ballot, but I'm on the ballot," Trump told one confidant who relayed the conversation to Axios. A Trump adviser told Axios: "Our job is to turn Trump voters into midterm voters." 🔎 The intrigue: The ads, however, are a de facto compromise between Trump and his political staff.

 "This is for the audience of one," Trump's confidant said, referring to the president. "If the boss wants it, he gets it." One Trump adviser said to expect more conventional ads: "We will be stripping the bark off of Democrats and supporting our candidates. ... It's not all Trump-focused ads." 🔎 Between the lines: Trump's message in his tax cut ads is good, Republican strategists say, but their research increasingly shows voters blame him for the war in Iran that's raising gas prices.

 "Diesel's at an all-time high . Gas is about $4 a gallon. We can't message that away," said another Republican who runs campaigns for a few federal candidates. 🔮 What's next: At a Rose Garden dinner Wednesday with dozens of House Republicans, Trump promised to campaign across the country in 35 races "to see if we can get everyone elected."

 If Trump wants to campaign for a candidate, it's going to be hard for them to say no, said one of the consultants involved in talks with MAGA Inc., the super PAC that backs Trump. "Say no to Trump, and he could punish you," the consultant said. "Say yes to him, and his presence might not help. This is the world we're in." Convention lineup ... Share this story . 

 
 
