 6. 🍽️ Your local gem 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The Astor, a 2,000-square-foot restaurant coming to Lower Manhattan, opens Sept. 15. Rendering: The Astor "The most anticipated openings in the U.S. this fall are taverns, bistros, pizzerias and intimate neighborhood spots serving the kind of food diners already know they like," Bloomberg's Kat Odell writes . 

 Why it matters: Every restaurant wants to be your new neighborhood spot. 🥩 Case in point: New York City heavyweight chef Andrew Carmellini is opening The Astor at Lower Manhattan's Public Theater this month.

 The Astor will serve American classics like deviled eggs and pigs-in-a-blanket. The average check is expected to be $75 a person. At the 2023 opening of another restaurant by the same chef, Café Carmellini, a single dish could cost that much. Other big-time restaurateurs are also in on the trend:

 Three-Michelin-starred Eleven Madison Park's Daniel Humm is opening Humm, a casual West Village spot, in October. Michael Rafidi, chef of D.C.'s Michelin-starred Albi, is opening Bizzeria in Georgetown in November to serve up sourdough pies and mezze. "I like the idea of giving more accessibility to the community," Rafidi told Bloomberg. Gift link . 

 
 
